From e62fafd1d5be6f1138f3d74f67ab30e0990b7275 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Finn Freas <finn.freas@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 01:09:59 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] router2: hold the routes inside a region to it

A Region has carried constr_wires/constr_pips, a wires pool and piplocs
since the kernel grew regions, but no router read them.  router2 now
does: an arc whose driver and sink cells are both in a region with
constr_pips (or constr_wires) may only use the pips at its piplocs (and
the wires in its pool).  The check sits in route_arc's forward and
backward expansion next to the reserved-wire check, so it holds in the
bounding-box search and in the unbounded retry alike; an arc that has no
route inside its region fails with an error naming the region instead of
leaving it.  Seeds from the net's existing routing are only taken when
their route back to the source stays inside, routing already present that
breaks the rule is ripped up unless it is locked, and locked routing that
breaks it is reported.

Arcs that cross a region's edge are not held.  With
region_contain_constants (default on) a constant net's arcs are held to
their sink's region.  --router2-region-keep-out keeps every other arc out
of a region that is neither its driver's nor its sink's; locked routing
is left alone.  Off by default.

xilinx: a pblock with EXCLUDE_PLACEMENT, or with CONTAIN_ROUTING true,
now holds its routing: its piplocs are the tiles of its sites and, beside
each, the interface and INT_L/INT_R tiles that serve it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
